From 10eec6a4ba13ce8392403c3c91bf87ddabffd382 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: a52sxq-kernel <a52sxq-kernel@example.invalid>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 17:39:56 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 3/7] arm64: kexec: machine_kexec() __nocfi

Both jumps in machine_kexec() are indirect calls to physical addresses;
with kCFI the type-hash load in front of the target faults in the
trampoline mapping. Found on the A52s with ACK 6.18 (CONFIG_CFI=y). No
effect on builds without CFI.
---
 arch/arm64/kernel/machine_kexec.c | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/kernel/machine_kexec.c b/arch/arm64/kernel/machine_kexec.c
index 8f9bc2327..e953905ca 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/kernel/machine_kexec.c
+++ b/arch/arm64/kernel/machine_kexec.c
@@ -160,7 +160,14 @@ int machine_kexec_post_load(struct kimage *kimage)
  *
  * Called from the core kexec code for a sys_reboot with LINUX_REBOOT_CMD_KEXEC.
  */
-void machine_kexec(struct kimage *kimage)
+/*
+ * __nocfi: both jumps below are indirect calls to PHYSICAL addresses (the idmap copy of
+ * cpu_soft_restart, the relocation trampoline copied to kimage->arch.kern_reloc). With kCFI
+ * the compiler reads the type hash from the word in front of the target; that word is not
+ * mapped in the trampoline page tables, so the check itself faults ("Unable to handle kernel
+ * paging request at ...ffc" right after "Bye!", seen on the A52s 2026-11-12 with ACK).
+ */
+__nocfi void machine_kexec(struct kimage *kimage)
 {
 	bool in_kexec_crash = (kimage == kexec_crash_image);
 	bool stuck_cpus = cpus_are_stuck_in_kernel();
